Let $M_0=4$, and suppose that $$4\le d=\lceil2\log_2m\rceil<m.$$ Let $a,b>0$, put $A=ma$, $B=mb$, and assume $$\label{eq:large-parameters}
\min(A,B)\ge20M_0d(d+1)^2.$$ There is $c_*>0$, depending only on $m,a,b$, with the following property. Suppose that $$\Psi:\{-1,1\}^{m\times m}\to\mathbb R,\qquad
f,g:\{-1,1\}^m\to\mathbb R$$ are odd, have sup norms at most $M_0$, and satisfy $$\label{eq:scalar-first}
\widehat f(e_i)=a,\qquad \widehat g(e_j)=b$$ together with the projection identities $$\label{eq:scalar-projections}
\begin{split}
\mathbb E_\sigma\sigma_i\Psi(\sigma S)&=a\,g(S_{i,:}),\\
\mathbb E_\tau\tau_j\Psi(S\tau)&=b\,f(S_{:,j})
\end{split}$$ for all $i,j,S$.

outputs.exact_statement:
Let $M_0=4$, and suppose that $$4\le d=\lceil2\log_2m\rceil<m.$$ Let $a,b>0$, put $A=ma$, $B=mb$, and assume $$\label{eq:large-parameters}
\min(A,B)\ge20M_0d(d+1)^2.$$ There is $c_*>0$, depending only on $m,a,b$, with the following property. Suppose that $$\Psi:\{-1,1\}^{m\times m}\to\mathbb R,\qquad
f,g:\{-1,1\}^m\to\mathbb R$$ are odd, have sup norms at most $M_0$, and satisfy $$\label{eq:scalar-first}
\widehat f(e_i)=a,\qquad \widehat g(e_j)=b$$ together with the projection identities $$\label{eq:scalar-projections}
\begin{split}
\mathbb E_\sigma\sigma_i\Psi(\sigma S)&=a\,g(S_{i,:}),\\
\mathbb E_\tau\tau_j\Psi(S\tau)&=b\,f(S_{:,j})
\end{split}$$ for all $i,j,S$. Then $$|\widehat\Psi(P)|\ge c_*$$ for some binary matrix $P$ of rank at least two.

Fix an integer $m\ge1$ and constants $D_g,D_f\ge1$. Suppose that $m$ and the parameters $a,b$ in <a href="#eq:lift-parameters" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:lift-parameters">[eq:lift-parameters]</a> satisfy the hypotheses of Lemma <a href="#lem:coefficient" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:coefficient">6</a>. There is $c_1\in(0,1/2]$, depending only on these parameters, such that the following holds. If $$c_m(G^*)\le D_g,\qquad c_m(F)\le D_f,\qquad 0\ne T:G\to F,$$ and $n>4m$ satisfies $$\label{eq:slow-decrease}
w_{n-2m}(T)\le2w_n(T),$$ then $$\label{eq:growth}
w_{2(n-2m)}(T)\ge c_1w_n(T).$$ The operator $T$ need not have finite rank.

Fix $m,D_g,D_f$ satisfying the assumptions of Lemma <a href="#lem:growth" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:growth">8</a>. There is an integer $n_0$, depending only on these parameters, such that every finite-rank $T:G\to F$ with $c_m(G^*)\le D_g$ and $c_m(F)\le D_f$ satisfies $$\label{eq:uniform-drop}
w_n(T)\le\tfrac12 w_{n-2m}(T)\qquad(n\ge n_0).$$ In particular there are numbers $\rho_N\to0$, independent of $G,F,T$, such that $$\label{eq:uniform-decay}
w_N(T)\le\rho_N\|T\|.$$ Both bounds also hold for every $T:G\to F$ that is an operator-norm limit of finite-rank maps, under the same moment hypotheses on $G^*$ and $F$.

Let $m\ge1$ be an integer, let $\Delta\ge1$, and let $X$ be a Banach space with $c_m(X^*)\le\Delta$. For every nonzero finite-dimensional subspace $E\subset X$, there exist a Banach space $G$, an injective compact operator $J:G\to X$, and a contraction $\iota:E\to G$, such that $$\label{eq:compact-domain}
J\iota x=x\quad(x\in E),\qquad
\|J\|\le2,\qquad c_m(G^*)\le12\Delta.$$

Let $N$ be a spreading invariant norm on $c_{00}$ for which merging adjacent entries is contractive. For every integer $n\geq1$, there are a finite stationary reversible marked chain and an odd labeling $g$ in a finite coordinate span such that $$N(g(e))=1\qquad\text{for every edge }e,$$ and, for the first $n$ edges $E_1,\ldots,E_n$ of the stationary chain, $$\label{eq:unit-walk}
 \mathbb P\left\{N\left(\sum_{i=1}^n g(E_i)\right)
                 \geq\frac n{24}\right\}\geq\frac3{20}.$$

Suppose $V$ is a normed space of Markov type $p$ with constant $K$. For a finite marked chain satisfying <a href="#eq:edge-balance" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:edge-balance">[eq:edge-balance]</a>, let $g:\mathcal E\to V$ satisfy $g(e^*)=-g(e)$. If $E_1,\ldots,E_n$ are its first $n$ edges, with initial state distributed as $\pi$, then $$\label{eq:odd-markov}
 \mathbb E\left\lVert \sum_{i=1}^n g(E_i)\right\rVert^p
 \leq K^p n\,\mathbb E\left\lVert g(E_1)\right\rVert^p.$$ The same conclusion holds if the finite span of the labels is finitely representable in a space of Markov type $p$ with constant $K$.

There are increasing homeomorphisms $h_s:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ such that $$\begin{aligned}
 h_s(y+1)&=h_s(y)+1, & h_{s^{-1}}&=h_s^{-1},\label{eq:periodic-action}\\
 h_s(\mathbb R\setminus\mathcal A_{s^{-1}})&=\mathcal A_s,
 & |h_s(y)-y|&<2.\label{eq:pingpong}
\end{aligned}$$ For a word $w=s_1\cdots s_k$, write $h_w=h_{s_1}\circ\cdots\circ h_{s_k}$, with $h_\varnothing$ the identity. If $w$ is nonempty and reduced, then $$\label{eq:interior}
 h_w(0)\in\operatorname{int}(\mathcal A_{s_1});$$ in particular, $h_w(0)\notin\mathbb Z$.
